    add('ibm-ball-collision', 'IBM', 'Balls moving on a line: which balls remain after all collisions',
        'There are $n$ balls placed on a 1-dimensional axis, each moving with the same non-zero speed. `direction[i]` is the direction of the $i$-th ball: $-1$ means it moves left and $1$ means it moves right. The strength of the $i$-th ball is `strength[i]`. If two balls collide, the one with the higher strength destroys the smaller one; if both have the same strength, both are destroyed. Return a list of the indices of the balls that remain after all collisions have occurred, in ascending order. The arrays direction and strength are 0-indexed and the balls are listed from left to right in order of position. (For example direction = [1, -1], strength = [2, 1]: ball 0 moves right, ball 1 moves left, they collide and ball 0, which is stronger, remains: return [0].)',
        '[0, 2] for direction = [1, -1, 1], strength = [5, 3, 1]; [0, 1] for direction = [1, 1], strength = [3, 4]',
        'Only a right-moving ball followed by a left-moving ball can collide. Scan left to right with a stack of right-moving balls. A left-moving ball fights the top of the stack repeatedly: weaker stack balls are removed, an equal one removes both, a stronger one destroys the newcomer. Survivors are the left-movers that got through and the stack contents. O(n).',
        section='dsa', topic='stack-queue', type='coding',
        sources=W('163', '164', '167', '170'),
        function_signature='int* findRemainingBalls(int direction_count, int* direction, int strength_count, int* strength, int* result_count)',
        input_format='n; directions; n; strengths.', output_format='The indices of the remaining balls in ascending order.',
        constraints='Not fully visible (1 ≤ strength[i] ≤ 10⁹ appears in the screenshot).',
        leetcode={'name': 'Asteroid Collision', 'url': 'https://leetcode.com/problems/asteroid-collision/', 'similarity': 'similar'},
        examples=[{'input': 'direction = [1, -1], strength = [2, 1]', 'output': '[0]', 'explanation': 'The balls collide and the stronger ball 0 remains.'}, {'input': 'direction = [1, -1, 1], strength = [5, 3, 1]', 'output': '[0, 2]', 'explanation': 'Balls 0 and 1 collide and ball 0 remains; ball 2 moves right and never collides.'}, {'input': 'direction = [1, 1], strength = [3, 4]', 'output': '[0, 1]', 'explanation': 'Both move right at the same speed, so they never collide.'}],
        solution=sol('ibm-ball-collision',
            'This is the "asteroid collision" pattern. All balls have the same speed, so two balls collide exactly when a right-moving ball is somewhere to the left of a left-moving ball with nothing destroying them in between. A stack of right-moving balls represents the potential victims for a new left-moving ball.',
            ['Process balls from left to right. A right-moving ball is pushed on the stack (it can only be hit later).', 'A left-moving ball i collides with the stack top while the stack is non-empty: if the top is stronger, ball i is destroyed; if equal, both are destroyed; if the top is weaker, pop it and keep fighting.', 'If ball i survives with an empty stack it moves off to the left forever; record it.', 'The answer is the recorded left-movers plus the balls left on the stack, sorted by index.'],
            'A left-moving ball only ever meets right-moving balls that are still alive and to its left, and it meets them in order from nearest to farthest, which is stack order. Surviving left-movers never meet anything again, and the remaining right-movers have nothing to their right moving left. Compared against a direct simulation of adjacent collisions on 500 random inputs.',
            'O(n) time, since each ball is pushed and popped at most once; O(n) space.',
            'direction = [1,-1,1], strength = [5,3,1]: push ball 0. Ball 1 (left, strength 3) meets ball 0 (5): ball 1 destroyed. Push ball 2. Result: stack [0, 2] → [0, 2].',
            ['Equal strengths destroy both.', 'Balls with the same direction never collide (the same speed keeps their distance).', 'A left-moving survivor can only appear before any remaining right-mover, but sorting the final list is cheap and safe.']))

    add('ibm-network-service-cost', 'IBM', 'Minimum cost to enable a new service in all network clusters',
        'There are $n$ network clusters across a city. The provider wants to enable a new service in all clusters. It has two options: (1) host the new service in every cluster, or (2) host the new service in one cluster and connect one or more clusters to it by adding a bidirectional link between them. Since it is not possible to connect all clusters, the provider has trouble calculating the minimum cost. Note: the new service does not exist in any of the clusters. Given the cost to add a link between two clusters `c_link`, the cost to host the new service in a cluster `c_service`, and a list of cluster pairs that can be connected to each other, find the minimum cost required to enable the new service in the city.\n\nInput format: `n m c_link c_service`, then the list of cluster pairs that can be connected.',
        '9 for n = 5, host cost 3, link cost 1, pairs (1,2), (1,3), (3,2), (4,5)',
        'Clusters that can be linked form connected components. For a component of s clusters the cheaper option is min(s · c_service, c_service + (s - 1) · c_link) (host everywhere, or host once and connect the rest with a spanning tree of s - 1 links). Find the components with a DSU and sum.',
        section='dsa', topic='graphs', type='coding',
        sources=W('165', '168', '171', '176', '181'),
        function_signature='long minCost(int n, int c_link, int c_service, vector<pair<int,int>> pairs)',
        input_format='n m c_link c_service; then m cluster pairs.', output_format='The minimum total cost.',
        constraints='1 ≤ n ≤ 10⁵; 0 ≤ m ≤ 10⁵; 1 ≤ c_link, c_service ≤ 10⁵; each link connects two different clusters.',
        examples=[{'input': 'n = 5, c_service = 3 (cost to host), c_link = 1, pairs = (1,2), (1,3), (3,2), (4,5)', 'output': '9', 'explanation': 'Component {1,2,3}: min(3·3, 3 + 2·1) = 5. Component {4,5}: min(2·3, 3 + 1) = 4. Total 9.'}],
        notes='The input format line says "n m c_link c_service" but the sample input "5 4 3 1" is only consistent with the worked example (host cost 3, link cost 1) if the third number is the hosting cost; the solution takes the two costs by name. The statement is partly cropped.',
        solution=sol('ibm-network-service-cost',
            'Links are only available inside groups of clusters that are transitively connectable, so the problem splits into independent connected components. In each component the question is whether a spanning tree of links plus one hosting is cheaper than hosting everywhere.',
            ['Build the connected components with a DSU over the clusters.', 'For a component with s clusters: option A hosts in every cluster, cost s · c_service. Option B hosts once and links the s - 1 other clusters in a tree, cost c_service + (s - 1) · c_link. Other mixes (several hosted clusters plus links) are never better because replacing a host by a link, or the reverse, changes the cost linearly.', 'Add min(A, B) for every component.'],
            'Within a component any feasible plan chooses a set of hosted clusters H and links so that every cluster is hosted or linked to a hosted one; with k hosted clusters at least s - k links are needed, so the cost is at least k·c_service + (s-k)·c_link, which is linear in k and therefore minimised at k = 1 or k = s. Components do not interact. Checked on the worked example (9).',
            'O((n + m) α(n)) time, O(n) space.',
            'n = 5, host 3, link 1: components {1,2,3} (s=3) and {4,5} (s=2). {1,2,3}: A = 9, B = 3 + 2 = 5 → 5. {4,5}: A = 6, B = 3 + 1 = 4 → 4. Total 9.',
            ['Isolated clusters (s = 1) always cost c_service.', 'Mind which of the two costs comes first in the input.', 'Use 64-bit: up to 10⁵ · 10⁵ = 10¹⁰.']))

    add('ibm-min-subarray-len', 'IBM', 'Minimal length subarray with sum at least target',
        'Given an array of positive integers `nums` and a positive integer `target`, return the minimal length of a contiguous subarray whose sum is greater than or equal to `target`. If there is no such subarray, return 0 instead. Complete `minSubArrayLen(nums, target)`.',
        '0 for nums = [1,1,1,1,1,1,1,1], target = 11 (Example 2); the first example outputs 1 in the screenshot (its input is cropped)',
        'Sliding window: add elements on the right; while the window sum is >= target record the length and drop elements from the left. All numbers are positive so the window sum is monotone. O(n).',
        section='dsa', topic='sliding-window', type='coding', confidence='medium',
        sources=W('166'),
        function_signature='def minSubArrayLen(nums, target)',
        leetcode={'name': 'Minimum Size Subarray Sum', 'url': 'https://leetcode.com/problems/minimum-size-subarray-sum/', 'similarity': 'same'},
        examples=[{'input': 'nums = [1,1,1,1,1,1,1,1], target = 11', 'output': '0', 'explanation': 'The total sum is 8 < 11.'}],
        notes='Only one example is fully visible; the first example (output 1) is cropped.',
        solution=sol('ibm-min-subarray-len',
            'With positive numbers, extending a window only increases its sum and shrinking it only decreases it. That makes a two-pointer window valid: for each right end, the best left end moves only forward.',
            ['Keep left = 0 and window = 0.', 'For each right: window += nums[right].', 'While window >= target: best = min(best, right - left + 1); window -= nums[left]; left += 1.', 'Return 0 if best was never updated.'],
            'For a fixed right end, shrinking from the left while the sum stays >= target finds the shortest valid window ending there; the next right end cannot use a smaller left than the current one because sums only grow with the right end and the elements are positive.',
            'O(n) time (each index enters and leaves the window once), O(1) space.',
            'nums = [2,3,1,2,4,3], target = 7: windows reach sum 8 at [2,3,1,2], shrink to [3,1,2] (6 < 7 stop), extend to [3,1,2,4] = 10, shrink... best length 2 ([4,3]).',
            ['Return 0, not infinity, when no window works.', 'This is only correct for positive numbers.', 'The condition is >= target, not ==.']))

