    add('ibm-domino-painting', 'IBM', 'Count ways to colour a 2×n domino tiling with 3 colours, adjacent dominoes different',
        'A valid domino arrangement is given using two strings in the array `domino`. All dominoes are unique (of size 1×2 or 2×1) and each domino is represented by an English character (lowercase or uppercase). Each domino can be coloured with one of Red, Green or Blue. Find the number of distinct ways to colour the dominoes so that no two adjacent dominoes have the same colour. Both halves of a domino have one colour. Return the answer modulo $10^9+7$.\n\nExample: domino = ["baa", "bcc"] is arranged as a vertical domino b followed by two horizontal dominoes a and c. The valid colourings are [RGG,RBB], [RBB,RGG], [GRR,GBB], [GBB,GRR], [BGG,BRR], [BRR,BGG], so the answer is 6.',
        '6 for ["baa","bcc"]; 6 for ["ab","ab"]; 12 for ["aacx","ddcx"]',
        'A 2×n tiling consists of vertical dominoes (one column) and pairs of stacked horizontal dominoes (two columns). Count colourings column block by block: first block V = 3, H = 6; transitions V→V 2, V→H 2, H→V 1, H→H 3. Multiply along the tiling. O(n).',
        section='dsa', topic='dp', type='coding',
        sources=W('174', '183', '187', '191'),
        function_signature='int countDistinctColorings(vector<string> domino)',
        input_format='The array has 2 strings of equal length.', output_format='The number of colourings modulo 10⁹+7.',
        constraints='The size of the array is 2 and both strings have equal length (the length bound is partly cropped); characters are letters.',
        examples=[{'input': 'domino = ["ab", "ab"]', 'output': '6', 'explanation': 'Two vertical dominoes next to each other: 3 · 2 = 6.'}, {'input': 'domino = ["baa", "bcc"]', 'output': '6', 'explanation': 'Three mutually adjacent dominoes: 3! = 6.'}, {'input': 'domino = ["aacx", "ddcx"]', 'output': '12', 'explanation': 'a and d (horizontal, stacked), then vertical c and x: 6 · 1 · 2 = 12.'}],
        solution=sol('ibm-domino-painting',
            'In a 2×n domino tiling, every column is either a vertical domino or half of a pair of horizontal dominoes stacked on top of each other (staggered horizontals cannot occur). So the picture is a sequence of blocks of widths 1 (V) and 2 (H = two dominoes).',
            ['Scan the columns: if top[i] = bottom[i] the block is V (width 1), otherwise it is H (width 2, covering columns i and i+1).', 'First block: V has 3 colours, H has 3·2 = 6 (the two stacked dominoes touch).', 'Transitions (the neighbours that touch): V→V: new colour differs from the previous one: 2. V→H: both new dominoes touch the V and each other: 2·1 = 2. H→V: the V touches both stacked dominoes of different colours: 1. H→H: top is not equal to the old top, bottom not equal to the old bottom, top ≠ bottom: 3 ways (checked by enumeration).', 'Multiply the factors along the blocks, modulo 10⁹+7.'],
            'The adjacency graph of the dominoes only links dominoes in neighbouring blocks (and the two stacked horizontals), so the number of valid colours of a block depends only on the colours of the previous block, which depends only on its type. For H→H, if the new top uses the old bottom colour the new bottom has 2 options, else it has 1: 2·... total 3. Compared against brute force over all 3^pieces colourings for random tilings.',
            'O(n) time, O(1) extra space.',
            '["aacx","ddcx"]: columns 0-1 differ (a/d) → H: 6. Column 2 (c/c) → V after H: ×1. Column 3 (x/x) → V after V: ×2. Total 12.',
            ['Take the modulus after every multiplication.', 'Upper and lower case letters are distinct dominoes.', 'Do not treat the string characters themselves as colours.']))

    add('ibm-youngest-missing-member', 'IBM', 'Youngest member whose age is missing from the age list',
        'You are given an array `age` of N ages and an array `member` of M strings of the form "Name,LastName,Age". The task is to find the name of the youngest missing member from the array age (a member is missing if his or her age is not present in age). Notes: (1) if all ages are provided, return null; (2) ages might not be in ascending order; (3) if more than one youngest member is missing, return the first occurrence.',
        '"Leena" for ages = [1,2,3,4,5] and the 10 members Ram,D,1; Dev,B,2; Adam,Jobs,3; Adam,Jobs,4; Ema,K,5; Leena,Jack,7; Patric,Queen,8; Liam,Jones,9; Riya,N,10; Dilip,K,22',
        'Put the given ages in a set. Scan the members in order, parse the age, skip those whose age is in the set, and keep the one with the smallest age (strictly smaller keeps the first occurrence on ties). Return its first name or null. O(N + M).',
        section='dsa', topic='hashing', type='coding', confidence='medium',
        sources=W('175', '193'),
        function_signature='string findYoungestMember(vector<int> ages, vector<string> members)',
        input_format='N and the ages; M and the member strings.', output_format='The name of the youngest missing member, or null.',
        constraints='Not visible (the screenshot also shows a custom-input error about array sizes).',
        examples=[{'input': 'ages = [1,2,3,4,5]; members = Ram,D,1; Dev,B,2; Adam,Jobs,3; Adam,Jobs,4; Ema,K,5; Leena,Jack,7; Patric,Queen,8; Liam,Jones,9; Riya,N,10; Dilip,K,22', 'output': 'Leena', 'explanation': 'The members with ages 1-5 are present; among the rest the youngest is Leena (age 7).'}],
        solution=sol('ibm-youngest-missing-member',
            'A member is "missing" when no entry of the age array equals their age. So we need the member with the smallest age that is not in the age set; ties are broken by input order.',
            ['Convert ages to a hash set.', 'Iterate the members in order; split each string on commas to get the name and the integer age.', 'Skip members whose age is in the set; otherwise update the best (name, age) only if the age is strictly smaller than the current best.', 'Return the best name, or null (None) if nothing was missing.'],
            'Every missing member is considered once, and the strict comparison makes the first of equal ages win. Hashing gives O(1) membership.',
            'O(N + M) time, O(N) space.',
            'Set {1,2,3,4,5}. Members with ages 1-5 are skipped. Leena (7) becomes best; Patric 8, Liam 9, Riya 10, Dilip 22 are not smaller → "Leena".',
            ['Return null / None, not an empty string, when everyone is present.', 'The age is the third comma-separated field; names may repeat.', 'The ages array need not be sorted.']))

    add('ibm-binary-addition', 'IBM', 'Add two binary strings',
        'Given binary strings `str1` and `str2`, add them and return the sum as a binary string. Example 1: str1 = "100", str2 = "010" gives "110". Example 2: str1 = "111", str2 = "11" gives "1010". Constraints: 1 <= length of str1, str2 <= 1000.',
        '"110" for str1 = "100", str2 = "010"; "1010" for "111" + "11"',
        'Schoolbook addition from the least significant bit with a carry; output bits in reverse. O(max(n, m)).',
        section='dsa', topic='strings', type='coding',
        sources=W('190'),
        function_signature='string binAddition(string str1, string str2)',
        leetcode={'name': 'Add Binary', 'url': 'https://leetcode.com/problems/add-binary/', 'similarity': 'same'},
        constraints='1 ≤ |str1|, |str2| ≤ 1000',
        examples=[{'input': 'str1 = "100", str2 = "010"', 'output': '110'}, {'input': 'str1 = "111", str2 = "11"', 'output': '1010'}],
        notes='In Example 1 the screenshot does not show whether the answer keeps leading zeros; the solution strips them.',
        solution=sol('ibm-binary-addition',
            'Binary addition is decimal column addition in base 2: the column sum is 0..3, its low bit is the result bit and its high bit is the carry.',
            ['Use two pointers from the ends of the strings and carry = 0.', 'While a pointer is valid or carry is set: total = carry + digits; append total % 2; carry = total // 2.', 'Reverse the collected bits and strip leading zeros (keep "0" if the result is zero).'],
            'The column rule is the definition of positional addition, so the loop reproduces the exact sum. Verified against Python integer addition for 300 random pairs.',
            'O(max(n, m)) time and space.',
            '"111" + "11": columns (1+1) → 0 carry 1; (1+1+1) → 1 carry 1; (1+1) → 0 carry 1; final carry 1 → "1010".',
            ['Do not convert to integers: the strings can be long.', 'Handle different lengths and the final carry.', 'Strip leading zeros if the inputs have them.']))

    add('ibm-min-friends', 'IBM', 'Smallest possible size of the largest fully-connected friend group',
        'A group of software engineers wants to develop an algorithm for finding strongly connected groups among members of a social networking site. A group of people is considered to be strongly connected if each person knows every other person within the group. The engineers start with a smaller task first. If a network contains $n$ people and there are $m$ pairs of relationships connecting them, what is the minimum size of the largest strongly connected group in the network? Hint: think of the people as nodes in a graph and their relationships as edges. Complete `minFriends(numNodes, numEdges)`.',
        '2 for (3, 2); 4 for (4, 6); 3 for (5, 7)',
        'We may place the m edges so as to avoid large cliques. The largest edge count of an n-node graph with no clique bigger than r is the Turan number: edges of the complete balanced r-partite graph. The answer is the smallest r with m <= Turan(n, r). Binary search r. O(log n).',
        section='dsa', topic='math', type='coding', hard=True, confidence='medium',
        sources=W('188', '192', '195', '199', '203'),
        function_signature='long minFriends(int numNodes, int numEdges)',
        input_format='numNodes, numEdges.', output_format='The minimum size of the largest strongly connected group.',
        constraints='2 ≤ numNodes ≤ 10⁴ (partly legible); 1 ≤ numEdges ≤ numNodes(numNodes-1)/2.',
        examples=[{'input': 'numNodes = 2, numEdges = 1', 'output': '2', 'explanation': 'The only relationship joins both people.'}, {'input': 'numNodes = 3, numEdges = 2', 'output': '2', 'explanation': 'Edges 1-2 and 1-3 give largest group of size 2.'}, {'input': 'numNodes = 4, numEdges = 6', 'output': '4', 'explanation': 'Six edges on four nodes form a complete graph.'}, {'input': 'numNodes = 5, numEdges = 7', 'output': '3', 'explanation': 'Any 5-node graph with 7 edges has a triangle, and 3 is achievable.'}],
        notes='The wording "strongly connected group" is interpreted as a clique (everyone knows everyone), as the statement defines it; the four sample outputs agree with this reading.',
        solution=sol('ibm-min-friends',
            'We choose where the edges go to make the biggest clique as small as possible. By Turan\'s theorem the densest graph with no clique of size r+1 is the complete balanced r-partite graph, so the largest clique can be forced to at most r exactly when m <= T(n, r).',
            ['Define T(n, r) = number of edges of the complete r-partite graph on n nodes with parts as equal as possible: with q = n // r and rem = n % r, T = (n² - (r - rem)q² - rem(q+1)²) / 2.', 'If m = 0 the answer is 1.', 'T(n, r) increases with r (T(n, n) = n(n-1)/2). Binary search the smallest r in [1, n] with m <= T(n, r).'],
            'A graph with clique number at most r has at most T(n, r) edges (Turan), and the Turan graph itself has clique number exactly r, so m edges can be arranged with clique number <= r iff m <= T(n, r) (remove edges from the Turan graph if m is smaller). Verified against exhaustive enumeration of all edge sets for n <= 6.',
            'O(log n) time, O(1) space.',
            'n = 5, m = 7: T(5, 2) = 6 < 7. T(5, 3) with parts 2,2,1 = (25 - 4 - 4 - 1)/2 = 8 >= 7. Answer 3.',
            ['Use the balanced partition; unbalanced parts have fewer edges.', 'm = 0 gives 1, not 0.', 'Use 64-bit arithmetic for n² when n is large.']))

    add('ibm-min-complexity-level', 'IBM', 'Split lectures into days to minimise the sum of daily maximum complexity',
        'Alexis attends a software engineering conference with several lectures, each with a certain complexity. Alexis has planned the order of lectures and needs to schedule them into several days. The goal is to attend at least one lecture every day and to plan the lectures so as to minimise the overall complexity of the conference. Each day\'s complexity is the complexity of the most complex lecture attended that day. Given the complexity levels of the lectures, in order, and the number of days of the conference, what is the overall minimum complexity that can be achieved? Example: complexity = [30, 10, 40, 20, 50], days = 2 gives 80 (day 1: lectures 30 and 10, day 2: 40, 20, 50: max(30,10) + max(40,20,50) = 30 + 50).',
        '80 for complexity = [30, 10, 40, 20, 50], days = 2',
        'Split the sequence into exactly `days` consecutive non-empty groups to minimise the sum of the group maxima. DP: dp[d][i] = min over j of dp[d-1][j] + max(complexity[j..i-1]); extend j downwards while updating the running maximum. O(days · n²) = 300³.',
        section='dsa', topic='dp', type='coding', hard=True,
        sources=W('196', '200', '206', '210'),
        function_signature='int findMinComplexity(vector<int> complexity, int days)',
        input_format='n and the complexities; days.', output_format='The minimum total complexity.',
        constraints='1 ≤ n ≤ 300; 1 ≤ days ≤ n; 1 ≤ complexity[i] ≤ 10⁵.',
        leetcode={'name': 'Minimum Difficulty of a Job Schedule', 'url': 'https://leetcode.com/problems/minimum-difficulty-of-a-job-schedule/', 'similarity': 'same'},
        examples=[{'input': 'complexity = [30, 10, 40, 20, 50], days = 2', 'output': '80', 'explanation': 'max(30, 10) + max(40, 20, 50) = 80.'}],
        solution=sol('ibm-min-complexity-level',
            'The order is fixed and every day takes a contiguous block of lectures, so the schedule is a partition of the array into `days` non-empty segments. The cost of a segment is its maximum, and we minimise the sum of segment costs.',
            ['dp[d][i] = minimum cost to schedule the first i lectures over exactly d days; dp[0][0] = 0, everything else infinity.', 'For d from 1 to days and i from d to n: let the last day cover lectures j..i-1. Iterate j from i-1 down to d-1 keeping day_max = max(complexity[j..i-1]). dp[d][i] = min(dp[d-1][j] + day_max).', 'The answer is dp[days][n]; if days > n it is impossible (not allowed by the constraints).'],
            'Any valid schedule has a last day, which is a suffix block j..i-1, and the earlier lectures form a valid (d-1)-day schedule; so the recurrence enumerates all schedules. Verified against enumerating all cut positions for n <= 8.',
            'O(days · n²) time, O(days · n) space; about 2.7·10⁷ operations for n = days = 300.',
            '[30,10,40,20,50], days=2: dp[1][i] = prefix maxima: 30,30,40,40,50. dp[2][5] = min over j: j=1: dp[1][1] + max(10,40,20,50) = 30 + 50 = 80; j=2: dp[1][2] + max(40,20,50) = 30 + 50 = 80; j=3: 40 + 50 = 90; j=4: 40 + 50 = 90. Answer 80.',
            ['Every day needs at least one lecture: iterate j from d-1.', 'Use exactly `days` groups, not at most.', 'Reset the running maximum for each i.']))

    add('ibm-shader-loader', 'IBM', 'Minimise the longest run of the same GPU after at most k switches',
        'A game\'s shaders are rendered using two GPUs, a and b. A string s represents, for the i-th shader, which GPU is used: shader[i] = \'a\' means GPU a, \'b\' means GPU b. The idleness of this dual GPU system is the maximum number of shaders for which the same GPU is used consecutively. For shader = "aabbba", GPU a is used for 2 shaders, then GPU b for 3, then a for 1, so the idleness is 3. To reduce the idleness the following operation can be used at most `switchCount` times: select any index i of the string and flip it (a becomes b and vice versa). Find the minimum possible idleness that can be achieved by applying the operations optimally.',
        '2 for shader = "aabbbaaaa", switchCount = 2; 2 for shader = "aaaaa", switchCount = 1',
        'Binary search the target idleness L. The flips needed to make every run at most L: for L = 1 it is min(mismatches with "abab...", with "baba..."); for L >= 2 it is the sum over runs of floor(run / (L+1)) (flip every (L+1)-th character of a run). The smallest feasible L is the answer. O(n log n).',
        section='dsa', topic='binary-search', type='coding', hard=True,
        sources=W('215', '218', '222', '228'),
        function_signature='int findMinimumIdleness(string shader, int switchCount)',
        input_format='The string shader and switchCount.', output_format='The minimum idleness.',
        constraints='1 ≤ |shader| ≤ 2·10⁵; 1 ≤ switchCount ≤ |shader|; shader contains only \'a\' and \'b\'.',
        examples=[{'input': 'shader = "aabbbaaaa", switchCount = 2', 'output': '2', 'explanation': 'Switch shader[4] (1-based) and shader[7]: "aabababaa" has idleness 2.'}, {'input': 'shader = "aaaaa", switchCount = 1', 'output': '2', 'explanation': 'Flip the middle character: "aabaa".'}],
        solution=sol('ibm-shader-loader',
            'Flips only help by breaking long runs. If we want every run to be at most L, a run of length r needs the cheapest set of flips that cuts it into pieces of length <= L. The feasibility "flips needed <= switchCount" is monotone in L, so we can binary search.',
            ['Split the string into maximal runs of equal characters.', 'For L >= 2: in a run of length r, flipping every (L+1)-th character cuts it into pieces of at most L, using floor(r / (L+1)) flips; this uses the fewest flips for that run. Sum over all runs.', 'For L = 1 (alternate completely): compare the string with "abab..." and with "baba..." and take the smaller number of mismatches (flips can merge runs, so the run formula does not apply).', 'Binary search the smallest L in [1, longest run] with flipsNeeded(L) <= switchCount.'],
            'For L >= 2 a flip inside a run changes one character to the opposite letter; flipping the (L+1)-th, 2(L+1)-th, ... characters leaves the flipped letter isolated in the middle of its run (a flipped character that lands next to a neighbouring run of the other letter only extends that run by one, and the pieces stay at most L long when L >= 2), so the bound floor(r/(L+1)) is achievable and optimal (any piece of L+1 equal characters needs at least one flip inside it, and the runs are disjoint). L = 1 is special because flips can combine with neighbours. Verified by brute force over all flip subsets for strings up to length 10.',
            'O(n log n) time (each check is O(n)), O(n) space.',
            '"aabbbaaaa", k=2. L=1 needs 4+ flips (mismatch counts), L=2: run lengths 2,3,4 → floor(2/3)+floor(3/3)+floor(4/3) = 0+1+1 = 2 ≤ 2 ✓. L=1 infeasible → answer 2.',
            ['The L = 1 case needs the alternating-pattern count.', 'Binary search over [1, longest run]; L larger than the longest run is useless.', 'switchCount is an upper bound ("at most").']))

    add('ibm-valid-bst-iterative', 'IBM', 'Check if a binary tree is a BST without recursion',
        'Given a binary tree given as a level-order array of strings (with "null" for missing children), write a function to determine whether it is a binary search tree (BST). A BST is a binary tree in which for every node the left subtree contains only nodes with values less than the node\'s value and the right subtree contains only nodes with values greater than the node\'s value; both the left and right subtrees must also be binary search trees. How would you implement a solution to check if the given tree is a BST? (Do NOT use recursion.)',
        'true for ["2","1","3"]; false for ["5","1","4","null","null","3","6"]',
        'An in-order traversal of a BST visits values in strictly increasing order. Do the traversal iteratively with an explicit stack and compare each value with the previous one. O(n) time, O(h) stack.',
        section='dsa', topic='bst', type='coding',
        sources=W('186', '250', '251', '252') + ['_storage_emulated_0_WhatsApp_Media_WhatsApp Images_IMG-20240908-WA0245.jpg'],
        function_signature='bool is_valid_bst(vector<string> root)',
        input_format='The level-order array of strings.', output_format='true / false.',
        leetcode={'name': 'Validate Binary Search Tree', 'url': 'https://leetcode.com/problems/validate-binary-search-tree/', 'similarity': 'same'},
        constraints='Not visible.',
        examples=[{'input': 'root = ["2", "1", "3"]', 'output': 'true'}, {'input': 'root = ["5", "1", "4", "null", "null", "3", "6"]', 'output': 'false', 'explanation': 'The node 3 lies in the right subtree of 5 but is smaller than 5.'}],
        notes='The screenshots include the photographed iterative in-order solution and the level-order deserializer; examples are constructed because the statement does not show any.',
        solution=sol('ibm-valid-bst-iterative',
            'The BST property is equivalent to "the in-order sequence is strictly increasing". That turns the problem into a traversal that must be done without recursion, so we use an explicit stack.',
            ['Rebuild the tree from the level-order list: a queue of nodes; each dequeued node takes the next two entries as its children ("null" means none).', 'Iterative in-order: push the current node and go left until none; pop a node, compare its value with the previously visited value (must be strictly greater), then move to its right child.', 'Return false at the first violation, true otherwise.'],
            'In-order traversal of a BST visits left subtree, node, right subtree, so the values come out sorted; conversely if the in-order sequence is strictly increasing, then for every node all values in its left subtree precede it and are smaller, and all values in its right subtree follow it and are larger. Checked against a recursive range-based validator on 500 random trees.',
            'O(n) time, O(h) extra space for the stack (O(n) for the deserialised tree).',
            '["2","1","3"] → in-order 1, 2, 3: increasing → true. ["5","1","4","null","null","3","6"] → in-order 1, 5, 3, 4, 6: 3 after 5 → false.',
            ['Equal values are not allowed (strictly greater).', 'Comparing only a node with its children is wrong; the whole subtree range matters.', 'An empty tree (or "null" root) is a valid BST.']))

    add('ibm-ipv4-to-ipv6', 'IBM', 'Convert an IPv4 address to its IPv6 form',
        'Each device on a network has an IP address. IPv4 addresses are 32 bits and IPv6 addresses are 128 bits. IPv4 to IPv6 conversion works as follows. IPv4 addresses of the form 127.x.x.x (the loopback addresses) map to ::1. For any other address, convert the four octets to hexadecimal (two digits each, upper case), concatenate the first and second octets and the third and fourth, and print ::FFFF:XXXX:XXXX. For 192.168.10.92 the octets are C0, A8, 0A, 5C, so the IPv6 form is ::FFFF:C0A8:0A5C. Constraints: the IPv4 address has only 4 octets, each between 0 and 255; if the first octet is 127 it is a loopback address; the IPv6 address must be printed in uppercase. Complete `convertToIpv6(ipv4address)`, which prints the IPv6 equivalent.',
        '::FFFF:C0A8:0A5C for 192.168.10.92; ::1 for 127.0.0.1',
        'Split on dots; if the first octet is 127 print ::1; otherwise format each octet as two upper-case hex digits and print "::FFFF:" + o1o2 + ":" + o3o4. O(1).',
        section='dsa', topic='strings', type='coding',
        sources=S8('246', '248') + ['_storage_emulated_0_WhatsApp_Media_WhatsApp Images_IMG-20240908-WA0247.jpg'],
        function_signature='void convertToIpv6(String ipv4address)',
        input_format='One IPv4 address string.', output_format='The IPv6 address on one line.',
        constraints='Exactly 4 octets, each 0-255; uppercase output.',
        examples=[{'input': '192.168.10.92', 'output': '::FFFF:C0A8:0A5C'}, {'input': '127.0.0.1', 'output': '::1'}],
        solution=sol('ibm-ipv4-to-ipv6',
            'It is a formatting task: IPv4-mapped IPv6 addresses are "::FFFF:" followed by the 32 address bits written as two groups of four hex digits; loopback is a special case.',
            ['Split the string at "." and convert the octets to integers.', 'If the first octet is 127, output "::1".', 'Otherwise format every octet with "%02X" (two upper-case hex digits).', 'Output "::FFFF:" + hex(o1) + hex(o2) + ":" + hex(o3) + hex(o4).'],
            'Two octets form a 16-bit group, written as exactly four hex digits (leading zeros kept, so 0x0A5C and not A5C). The prefix ::FFFF: is the constant of the IPv4-mapped format.',
            'O(1) time and space.',
            '192.168.10.92 → C0 A8 0A 5C → "::FFFF:" + "C0A8" + ":" + "0A5C" = ::FFFF:C0A8:0A5C.',
            ['Pad each octet to two hex digits (0x0A, not A).', 'Upper-case letters are required or the tests fail.', 'Loopback applies to the whole 127.x.x.x range, not just 127.0.0.1.']))

    add('ibm-triangles-by-area', 'IBM', 'Sort triangles by area (Heron\'s formula)',
        'You are given $n$ triangles and their sides $a$, $b$ and $c$. Print them in the same style, but sorted by their areas from the smallest to the largest. It is guaranteed that all the areas are different. The area of a triangle with sides a, b, c is given by Heron\'s formula: $\\sqrt{p(p-a)(p-b)(p-c)}$ with $p = (a+b+c)/2$. Input: the first line contains $n$; then $n$ lines with three space-separated integers $a$, $b$, $c$. Constraints: $1 \\le n \\le 100$; $1 \\le a, b, c \\le 70$ and the triangle inequalities hold. Output: print exactly $n$ lines, each with the three sides.\n\nFor the input 3 triangles (7 24 25), (5 12 13), (3 4 5) the output is 3 4 5, 5 12 13, 7 24 25.',
        '3 4 5 / 5 12 13 / 7 24 25 for the sample',
        'Compare the squared area (times 16): (a+b+c)(-a+b+c)(a-b+c)(a+b-c), which is an integer, so sort the triangles by that key without floating point errors.',
        section='dsa', topic='sorting', type='coding',
        sources=S8('249', '250', '251'),
        function_signature='int sort_by_area(triangle* tr, int n)',
        input_format='n; then n lines a b c.', output_format='n lines a b c sorted by area.',
        constraints='1 ≤ n ≤ 100; 1 ≤ a, b, c ≤ 70; a + b > c, a + c > b, b + c > a.',
        examples=[{'input': '3\n7 24 25\n5 12 13\n3 4 5', 'output': '3 4 5\n5 12 13\n7 24 25', 'explanation': 'Areas 84, 30 and 6... printed from the smallest: 3-4-5 (area 6), 5-12-13 (30), 7-24-25 (84).'}],
        solution=sol('ibm-triangles-by-area',
            'Sorting is simple once we have a comparable key. Heron\'s formula under a square root is fine with floats, but the quantity 16·area² = (a+b+c)(-a+b+c)(a-b+c)(a+b-c) is an exact integer and has the same ordering.',
            ['For each triangle compute key = (a+b+c)(-a+b+c)(a-b+c)(a+b-c).', 'Sort the triangles by key ascending (the areas are all different, so no tie-breaking is needed).', 'Print the sides in the original order for each triangle.'],
            'Area = sqrt(key)/4, a strictly increasing function of key, so sorting by key sorts by area. Using integers avoids rounding problems when two areas are close.',
            'O(n log n) time, O(n) space.',
            'Keys 16·area²: (3,4,5): 12·6·4·2 = 576; (5,12,13): 30·20·6·4 = 14400; (7,24,25): 56·42·8·6 = 112896. Ascending order: 3 4 5, 5 12 13, 7 24 25.',
            ['Floating point comparisons can fail for close areas; the integer key cannot.', 'Print exactly n lines, with the sides in the original order.', 'In C, sort with qsort and a comparator on the key.']))

    add('ibm-play-segments', 'IBM', 'Minimum segments Player 1 must play to beat Player 2',
        'A video game developer is building a game in which the character moves through several segments of a level. In each segment, if the character collects a coin the player scores a point; if a segment does not contain a coin the player loses a point. Player 1 always begins the level, and then the game is turned over to Player 2 to complete the level. Player 1\'s goal is to achieve a higher score than Player 2 once the level is completed. Given the status of the game segments (whether they contain a coin or not), determine the minimum number of segments Player 1 should play so that in the end their score is greater than Player 2\'s score. Example: segments = [1, 1, 0, 1]. Playing 0 segments gives scores 0 vs 2; 1 segment gives 1 vs 1; 2 segments give 2 vs 0, so the answer is 2.',
        '2 for coins = [1, 1, 0, 1]; 0 for coins = [1, 0, 0, 1, 0]',
        'Map each segment to +1 (coin) or -1 (no coin). If Player 1 plays the first s segments, their score is the prefix sum P(s) and Player 2 gets total - P(s). Find the smallest s with P(s) > total - P(s). O(n).',
        section='dsa', topic='arrays', type='coding',
        sources=['IMG-20240717-WA0004.jpg', 'IMG-20240717-WA0005.jpg', 'IMG-20240717-WA0006.jpg', 'IMG-20240717-WA0007.jpg', 'IMG-20240717-WA0008.jpg', 'IMG-20240717-WA0009.jpg'],
        function_signature='int playSegments(vector<int> coins)',
        input_format='n and the coins array.', output_format='The minimum number of segments.',
        constraints='1 ≤ n ≤ 10⁵; coins[i] is 0 or 1.',
        examples=[{'input': 'coins = [1, 1, 0, 1]', 'output': '2', 'explanation': 'After 2 segments Player 1 has 2 and Player 2 has 1 - 1 = 0.'}, {'input': 'coins = [1, 0, 0, 1, 0]', 'output': '0', 'explanation': 'With 0 segments Player 1 has 0 and Player 2 has 2 - 3 = -1.'}],
        notes='What to return when no s works (e.g. all segments empty... that case is handled since a negative opponent total still helps) is not stated; the solution returns -1 in the impossible case.',
        solution=sol('ibm-play-segments',
            'The score is a prefix sum of ±1 values. Player 1 takes a prefix and Player 2 takes the remaining suffix, so for each prefix length s we can compare the two scores in O(1) after one pass.',
            ['Convert coins to +1 / -1 and compute total = sum of all.', 'Scan s from 0 to n with a running prefix P(s).', 'Return the first s with P(s) > total - P(s).'],
            'Player 1 plays the first s segments and Player 2 the rest, so the two scores are exactly P(s) and total - P(s). The first s satisfying the inequality is the minimum.',
            'O(n) time, O(1) space.',
            '[1,1,0,1]: values +1,+1,-1,+1, total 2. s=0: 0 > 2? no. s=1: 1 > 1? no. s=2: 2 > 0? yes → 2.',
            ['A coin gives +1 and an empty segment -1 (not 0).', 'Player 2\'s score can be negative, so s = 0 may already win.', 'Include s = 0 and s = n.']))

    add('ibm-min-jumps', 'IBM', 'Minimum jumps with "next element" or "jump nums[i]" moves',
        'Given a 0-indexed array of integers `nums`, your goal is to reach the last position from the first position, using minimal traversals. From index $i$ you may jump `nums[i]` positions forward, or you may choose to jump to the next element (that is, to position $i+1$). Find the minimum number of jumps to reach the last position. Constraints: $1 \\le$ nums.length $\\le 1000$; $0 \\le$ nums[i] $\\le 1000$.',
        None,
        'Model positions as graph nodes with edges i → i+1 and i → i+nums[i] (when inside the array). Every edge costs one jump, so a BFS from 0 gives the minimum number of jumps to n-1. O(n).',
        section='dsa', topic='graphs', type='coding', confidence='low',
        sources=['IMG-20240803-WA0002.jpg'],
        function_signature='int minJumps(int nums_count, int* nums)',
        constraints='1 ≤ nums.length ≤ 1000; 0 ≤ nums[i] ≤ 1000.',
        examples=[{'input': 'nums = [2, 3, 1, 1, 4]', 'output': '2', 'explanation': 'Constructed example: step 0 → 1 (next element), then jump nums[1] = 3 from index 1 to index 4: 2 jumps.'}],
        notes='No examples are shown; whether a jump may overshoot the last index is also not stated. The solution allows only landing inside the array.',
        solution=sol('ibm-min-jumps',
            'Each position has at most two outgoing moves of equal cost, so the shortest sequence of jumps is a shortest path in an unweighted graph.',
            ['Nodes 0..n-1; edges i → i+1 and i → i+nums[i] if the target index is below n.', 'BFS from node 0, storing the distance in jumps.', 'Return the distance of node n-1 (0 if n = 1).'],
            'BFS explores nodes in non-decreasing number of jumps, so the first time n-1 is reached the count is minimal. The step to i+1 guarantees reachability.',
            'O(n) time and space.',
            '[2,3,1,1,4]: from 0 the moves are to 1 and 2. From 1 (value 3) we can reach 4 in one more jump, so dist[4] = 2.',
            ['Both moves cost one jump.', 'Index i + nums[i] may exceed the array; ignore it unless overshooting is allowed.', 'nums[i] = 0 only leaves the "next element" move.']))

    add('ibm-subint-divisors', 'IBM', 'Count length-k substrings of an integer that divide the integer',
        'Given an integer $m$, define a *subint* as a contiguous substring of the digits of $m$ that is a divisor of $m$. Given two integers $m$ and $k$, find the number of subints of $m$ whose length is $k$ (count duplicates as well). Example: m = 500, k = 1 gives 1 (5 divides 500, while the digit 0 does not). m = 224, k = 2 gives 0 (22 and 24 are the substrings of length 2 and neither divides 224).',
        '1 for m = 500, k = 1; 0 for m = 224, k = 2',
        'Take the decimal string of m and every window of length k; convert it to an integer v, and count it if v > 0 and m % v == 0. O(len(m) · k).',
        section='dsa', topic='math', type='coding',
        sources=['IMG-20240803-WA0005.jpg'],
        function_signature='int numberOfSubint(int m, int k)',
        constraints='Not visible.',
        examples=[{'input': 'm = 500, k = 1', 'output': '1', 'explanation': 'Digits 5, 0, 0: 5 divides 500; 0 does not divide anything.'}, {'input': 'm = 224, k = 2', 'output': '0', 'explanation': '22 and 24 do not divide 224.'}],
        solution=sol('ibm-subint-divisors',
            'There are at most len(m) windows, so brute force is enough. The only subtlety is a window with value 0, which is never a divisor.',
            ['s = str(m); for each start i in 0..len(s)-k take v = int(s[i:i+k]).', 'Count the window if v != 0 and m % v == 0.', 'Return the count (duplicate substrings count separately).'],
            'The definition is checked literally for every window.',
            'O(len(m)) windows, each O(k), so tiny.',
            'm = 500, k = 1: windows 5, 0, 0 → only 5 divides 500 → 1.',
            ['Skip v = 0 to avoid division by zero.', 'Substrings with leading zeros (such as "05") are interpreted by value (5); the statement does not say otherwise.', 'k greater than the number of digits gives 0.']))

    add('ibm-balanced-or-not', 'IBM', 'Can each <> expression be balanced with a limited number of replacements?',
        'Consider a string that consists of the characters `<` and `>` only. The string is balanced if each `<` always appears before (to the left of) a corresponding `>`; they need not be adjacent. Each `<` and `>` acts as a unique pair of symbols, and neither symbol can be considered part of any other pair. To balance a string, any `>` character can be replaced by `<>`. Given an expression and a maximum number of replacements, determine whether the string can be balanced. Process a series of expressions and their corresponding maxReplacements and return an array whose i-th element is 1 if expressions[i] can be balanced and 0 otherwise.\n\nExample: for expressions = ["<<>><><><>", ">>", ...] the first strings are already balanced; ">>" can be balanced in two moves by replacing each > with <> to make <><>.',
        '[1, 0] for expressions = ["<>>>", "<>>>>"], maxReplacements = [2, 2]',
        'Scan left to right with a counter of unmatched `<`. A `>` with an open `<` closes it for free; a `>` with none open is unmatched and must be replaced by `<>` (cost 1). At the end any still-open `<` makes it impossible (replacing > never adds an unmatched-closer). Balanced iff open == 0 and replacements <= max. O(length).',
        section='dsa', topic='stack-queue', type='coding',
        sources=S15('188', '189', '191', '192', '193', '194', '195', '196'),
        function_signature='vector<int> balancedOrNot(vector<string> expressions, vector<int> maxReplacements)',
        input_format='n; the expressions; n; the maxReplacements.', output_format='n integers, 1 or 0.',
        constraints='1 ≤ n ≤ 10²; 1 ≤ |expressions[i]| ≤ 10⁵; 0 ≤ maxReplacements[i] ≤ 10⁵.',
        examples=[{'input': 'expressions = ["<>>>", "<>>>>"], maxReplacements = [2, 2]', 'output': '[1, 0]', 'explanation': '"<>>>" needs 2 replacements; "<>>>>" needs 3. '}, {'input': 'expressions = ["<<<><><>>"], maxReplacements = [2]', 'output': '[0]', 'explanation': 'There are more < than > so it can never be balanced.'}],
        solution=sol('ibm-balanced-or-not',
            'It is a bracket matching variant where `<` opens and `>` closes. The special operation `>` → `<>` converts one closer into a self-contained matched pair, which is the only way to repair a closer that has nothing to pair with.',
            ['Scan the string keeping open = number of unmatched `<`.', 'On `<`: open += 1. On `>`: if open > 0 then open -= 1 (it pairs with an earlier `<`), else it is an unmatched closer and we must spend one replacement (need += 1).', 'After the scan, the string can be balanced iff open == 0 (no `<` left without a partner) and need <= maxReplacements. Output 1 or 0 accordingly.'],
            'A leftover `<` can never be fixed: the operation only creates new matched pairs and cannot supply a `>` to the right of an unmatched `<`. Each unmatched `>` needs its own replacement, and a replacement fixes exactly one such `>`. So the minimum number of replacements is the count of unmatched closers when the opens are exhausted. Compared against brute force over all replacement subsets on 500 random strings.',
            'O(total length) time, O(1) extra space.',
            '"<>>>": `<` open=1; first `>` closes it; the next two `>` have no open `<`: need = 2 ≤ 2 and open = 0 → 1. "<>>>>": need = 3 > 2 → 0.',
            ['An unmatched `<` at the end means impossible regardless of the limit.', 'Matching `>` with an earlier `<` is free; only unmatched `>` cost.', 'maxReplacements can be 0.']))

    add('ibm-max-value-sum', 'IBM', 'Maximise the value sum after decrypting one window of k files',
        'There is a list of encrypted files of size $n$, each with a different value, and a list of $n$ binary values. A value of 1 represents a file that has been decrypted, and 0 represents an encrypted file that is not yet decrypted. The value sum is the sum of the values of the files whose binary entry is 1. In a single operation, a group of up to $k$ encrypted files can be decrypted simultaneously; a group is a contiguous segment (subarray) of the array. Given the file values, the binary list and $k$, find the maximum possible value sum.',
        '16 for encrypted_file = [1, 3, 5, 2, 5, 4], binary = [1, 1, 0, 1, 0, 0], k = 3',
        'The base sum is the sum of values with binary = 1. Decrypting a window adds the values of the zeros in it. Since all values are positive, the best window has length exactly k: use a sliding window of the "gain" array (value if binary is 0 else 0). Answer = base + best window sum. O(n).',
        section='dsa', topic='sliding-window', type='coding',
        sources=S15('205', '206', '207'),
        function_signature='int getMaxValueSum(vector<int> encrypted_file, vector<int> binary, int k)',
        input_format='n and the values; n and the binary values; k.', output_format='The maximum value sum.',
        constraints='1 ≤ k ≤ n ≤ 10⁵; 1 ≤ encrypted_file[i] ≤ 10⁴; binary[i] is 0 or 1.',
        examples=[{'input': 'encrypted_file = [1, 3, 5, 2, 5, 4], binary = [1, 1, 0, 1, 0, 0], k = 3', 'output': '16', 'explanation': 'Decrypt the 3rd to 5th files: binary becomes [1,1,1,1,1,0] and the value sum is 1+3+5+2+5 = 16.'}],
        solution=sol('ibm-max-value-sum',
            'Decrypting a file with binary 1 changes nothing, and a window of k covers k files, so the gain of a window is just the sum of the values of its 0-entries. Positive values mean a longer window never hurts, so windows of exactly k are enough.',
            ['base = sum of values where binary = 1.', 'gain[i] = value[i] if binary[i] = 0 else 0.', 'Slide a window of length k over gain, tracking the maximum window sum.', 'Return base + max window sum.'],
            'The final value sum is base plus the sum of the gains inside the chosen window. Maximising over all windows of length k (an "up to k" window is dominated by extending it to k, because gains are non-negative) gives the optimum.',
            'O(n) time, O(n) space (or O(1) with a running window).',
            'values [1,3,5,2,5,4], binary [1,1,0,1,0,0], k=3: base = 1+3+2 = 6. gain = [0,0,5,0,5,4]. Windows of length 3: [0,0,5]=5, [0,5,0]=5, [5,0,5]=10, [0,5,4]=9 → best 10. Answer 6 + 10 = 16.',
            ['The window is contiguous.', 'Windows can be shorter than k only if n < k (not possible here).', 'Use 64-bit sums if the values are larger.']))

    add('ibm-email-regex', 'IBM', 'Write a regular expression for hackerrank.com emails',
        'Valid HackerRank emails are of the form user@hackerrank.com, and the characteristics of user are: (1) it starts with 1 to 6 lowercase English letters denoted by the character class [a-z]; (2) the lowercase letters are followed by an optional underscore, i.e. zero or one occurrence of the underscore character; (3) the optional underscore is followed by 0 to 4 optional digits denoted by the character class [0-9]. Complete the code in the editor by replacing the blank with a regular expression that matches valid email addresses according to the criteria above. The locked code prints True for each correct match and False for each incorrect match. An example of a valid email is abcdef1234@hackerrank.com. The address a7_1@baddomain.com fails for two reasons: digits cannot precede the underscore, and the domain is not hackerrank.com.\n\nExample queries: julia@hackerrank.com, julia_@hackerrank.com, julia_0@hackerrank.com, julia0_@hackerrank.com, julia@gmail.com → True, True, True, False, False.',
        '^[a-z]{1,6}_?[0-9]{0,4}@hackerrank\\.com$',
        'Build the pattern piece by piece: [a-z]{1,6} for the letters, _? for the optional underscore, [0-9]{0,4} for the digits, then @hackerrank\\.com. Anchor with ^ and $ so the whole string must match (and escape the dot).',
        section='cs', topic='general-cs', type='subjective',
        sources=S15('208', '209', '210', '211', '212', '213'),
        function_signature='regex regularExpression("...")',
        constraints='1 ≤ query ≤ 10³; 1 ≤ string length ≤ 10³.',
        examples=[{'input': 'julia@hackerrank.com', 'output': 'True'}, {'input': 'julia_@hackerrank.com', 'output': 'True'}, {'input': 'julia_0@hackerrank.com', 'output': 'True'}, {'input': 'julia0_@hackerrank.com', 'output': 'False', 'explanation': 'A digit may not precede the underscore.'}, {'input': 'julia@gmail.com', 'output': 'False'}],
        solution=sol('ibm-email-regex',
            'Translate each rule of the specification into a regex fragment in the order the characters appear: letters, optional underscore, optional digits, then the fixed domain.',
            ['Letters: [a-z]{1,6}', 'Optional underscore: _?', 'Optional digits: [0-9]{0,4} (so "julia_" and "julia" are valid and "julia_0" too)', 'Domain: @hackerrank\\.com (escape the dot, otherwise it matches any character)', 'Anchor the pattern: ^ ... $ (or use regex_match, which requires a full match).'],
            'Each fragment accepts exactly the strings allowed by its rule, and because they are concatenated in the string order the whole pattern accepts exactly the valid emails. The digits come after the optional underscore, which explains why julia0_ is rejected: after "julia" the pattern would need to see "_" or "@" or digits, and with digits first the underscore cannot follow.',
            'Matching is linear in the length of the string.',
            '"julia0_@hackerrank.com": [a-z]{1,6} matches "julia", _? matches nothing, [0-9]{0,4} matches "0", then the next character "_" is not "@" → no match. "julia_0@hackerrank.com" matches "julia", "_", "0", "@hackerrank.com" → True.',
            ['Without anchors a longer string containing a valid email would match.', 'Escape the dot in the domain.', 'Digits may appear without an underscore (abcdef1234@hackerrank.com is valid).']))
    # Note: the check for this regex is in build/solution_checks/ibm-email-regex.py (sol() is not used for a regex answer layout)

    add('ibm-almost-equivalent', 'IBM', 'Almost equivalent string pairs (letter counts differ by at most 3)',
        'Two strings are considered *almost equivalent* if they have the same length and for each lowercase letter x, the number of occurrences of x in the two strings differs by no more than 3. There are two arrays of $n$ strings, s and t. Strings s[i] and t[i] make the i-th pair. For each pair, determine if they are almost equivalent. Return an array of n strings, either "YES" or "NO", one for each pair.\n\nExample: s = ["aabaab", "aaaaabb"], t = ["bbabbc", "abb"]. For the first pair the counts of a, b, c differ by 3, 2, 1, so it is YES; in the second pair the count of "a" differs by 4, so the answer is NO. The return array is ["YES", "NO"].',
        '["YES"] for s = ["aaa"], t = ["aab"]',
        'For each pair check equal length, then count letters of both strings and verify that every letter count differs by at most 3. O(total length).',
        section='dsa', topic='strings', type='coding',
        sources=S15('214', '215', '216', '217', '218'),
        function_signature='vector<string> areAlmostEquivalent(vector<string> s, vector<string> t)',
        input_format='n; the strings s; n; the strings t.', output_format='n lines YES or NO.',
        constraints='1 ≤ n ≤ 5; 1 ≤ length of any string ≤ 10⁵.',
        examples=[{'input': 's = ["aaa"], t = ["aab"]', 'output': '["YES"]', 'explanation': 'Only a and b occur; the counts differ by 1 for each.'}, {'input': 's = ["aabaab", "aaaaabb"], t = ["bbabbc", "abb"]', 'output': '["YES", "NO"]', 'explanation': 'In the second pair the letter a appears 5 times versus 1 (difference 4).'}],
        solution=sol('ibm-almost-equivalent',
            'Equivalence is decided by the letter-frequency difference only, so a 26-element counter per pair is enough.',
            ['If len(s[i]) != len(t[i]) the answer is NO.', 'Otherwise build diff[26]: add 1 for each letter of s[i], subtract 1 for each letter of t[i].', 'The answer is YES iff |diff[c]| <= 3 for all letters, else NO.'],
            'The condition in the statement is exactly the bound on each letter count difference; the diff array holds these differences.',
            'O(sum of lengths) time, O(26) extra space.',
            's = "aaa", t = "aab": diff[a] = 3 - 2 = 1, diff[b] = 0 - 1 = -1 → all within 3 → YES.',
            ['Check the lengths first.', 'Only lowercase letters are used; the difference is absolute.', 'Return "YES"/"NO" strings, not booleans.']))
